      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "![graph](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/2024_05_01_0b803e7c47808b53c351g-014.jpg?height=250&width=540&top_left_y=882&top_left_x=1427)\n\nTo determine which graphs of functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one. A function is one-to-one if every horizontal line intersects its graph at most once. This ensures that each \\(x\\)-value corresponds to exactly one \\(y\\)-value, which is a requirement for a function to have an inverse.\n\nLet's analyze each graph:\n\n1. **Graph A**: This graph is a parabola that opens upwards. It has a single point of intersection with a horizontal line, so every horizontal line touches the parabola at exactly one point. Therefore, Graph A is one-to-one.\n2. **Graph B**: This graph is a parabola that opens upwards but it has a vertex in the top left corner. If we draw a horizontal line through the vertex, it intersects the parabola at two points. Thus, \\(x = 0\\) is a point where the horizontal line hits the parabola twice. Therefore, Graph B is not one-to-one.\n3. **Graph C**: This graph is a straight line. Every horizontal line will intersect the line at exactly one point. Therefore, Graph C is one-to-one.\n4. **Graph D**: This graph is a quarter of a circle centered at the origin. Every horizontal line will intersect the circle at most once. Therefore, Graph D is one-to-one.\n5. **Graph E**: This graph is a sine function. The graph of \\(y = \\sin(x)\\) repeats its values over certain intervals, but when it is plotted over a period of \\(\\pi\\), the line \\(y = 0\\) intersects the graph at three points. Therefore, Graph E is not one-to-one.\n\nThe functions that have inverses are Graph A, Graph C, Graph D, and Graph E. Therefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{A, C, D, E}\\).",
